the towns here on the front lines in  eastern ukraine sit dangerously close to  the areas controlled by the  russian-backed separatists  this is marinka where life is divided in  two  there was a time before the war in 2014  when there was calm  now it's a place literally on the edge  the shells came out of nowhere  violations of the ceasefire  this video posted to social media claims  to show the terrifying moment as they  crashed onto the streets  this woman was hit by shrapnel her hand  badly injured  other areas along the dividing line also  experienced barrages of artillery before  they went quiet again  walking around these border towns it's a  very strange experience on the one hand  everything's normal there's cars  on the streets there's people around  there's houses there's shops open  but on the other hand  just about 500 meters away you have the  front lines and just beyond that the  russian-backed separatists  and what it means  is that the war is never far away  the principal of the local school fears  this could be the start of another  sustained round of fighting or worse  she shows me to the basements where  she'd led the children when the shelling  started  it's basic but safe  with sleeping bags water and medical  supplies  no one here knows where this conflict is  going for them this is another episode  in a war which has changed their lives  and town they hope peace will come one  day but are not expecting any miracles  alex rossi sky news marine corps
 

